      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": {
        "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
        "Label": "IMPLICATIONS",
        "content": "Neuropathic cancer pain caused by tumor infiltration in the sacral plexus is primarily treated by nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs, antidepressants, anticonvulsants, and opioids. In one patient with severe pain despite pharmacotherapy, a catheter for the continuous administration of local anesthetics was inserted along the first sacral root, resulting in markedly improved analgesia."
      }},
